<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">David Malo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1853">1853</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Davida Malo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Malo (Davida Malo) was a Native Hawaiian historian, educator, politician and minister of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. Born in Keauhou on the Island of Hawaiʻi, he grew up as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> united the islands and probably served the chief Kuakini as oral historian and court genealogist. In 1823 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahaina">Lahaina</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a>, learned to read and write from the Reverend William Richards, became a Christian and joined the first class of the Lahainaluna School. He composed a lament for Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ka-ahumanu">Kaʻahumanu</a> in 1832, was elected to the kingdom's first House of Representatives in 1841 and was ordained a minister in 1852. His history, Ka Moʻolelo Hawaiʻi, appeared in English in 1898.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most of what is known comes from the Hawaiian scholars <b>David Malo</b>, Samuel Kamakau and John Papa ʻĪʻī, writing after the kapu ended in 1819, and from European visitors’ journals.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li></ul></section>
